INT. CONSERVATORY WEDDING RECEPTION - EVENING

A Victorian glasshouse strung with paper lanterns. Ferns and fig trees crowd the corners. Rain drums on the panes. Tables laid with mismatched china.

The guests murmur around a small dance floor, six of them clustered near the cake: the bride's mother GWENDA FITZALAN-OKORO (60s, silver gown, brimming eyes), the groom's brother PIERS LANGLEY-ASANTE (30s, loosened tie), an old friend TABITHA NWOSU-REYES (40s, wine glass in each hand), the DJ (20s, headphones, bored), a caterer LUCIO MARCHETTI-DAS (50s, white jacket), and a STRANGER in a rain-soaked coat at the glass door.

The stranger has no card, no corsage, no seat. He looks at the dance floor as if at a stage he once knew.

GWENDA
Who is that?

PIERS
No idea.

TABITHA
He's very wet.

The stranger steps in. Dripping, he crosses the floor and stops before Gwenda.

STRANGER
Mrs. Fitzalan-Okoro. You won't remember me.

GWENDA
I don't believe I do.

STRANGER
Twenty-six years ago, I proposed to your daughter's mother. In this room.

Silence. The DJ takes off his headphones. Lucio sets down the tray.

GWENDA
(whisper)
Daniel.

DANIEL
Hello, Gwenda.

She puts a hand to her mouth. Behind her, the music trails away. Rain drums on the glass.
